There are also source values such as Index, Ontology, Governance, Documentation, Schema-Matter, Front-Matter, Research, and Validation.

This is a direct warning against treating every historical value as one ontological vocabulary. Some values describe what a thing is; others describe page role, system role, publication form, or governance/architecture function.

=== Evidence boundary ===

The <code>Has entity type</code>, <code>Has page type</code>, <code>Has domain</code>, Topic relations, and similar counts above are '''source-syntax evidence'''. V1 documents frequently embedded SMW examples/specifications in their bodies. The archive does not contain a complete exported semantic-store state proving that every source occurrence behaved as a deliberate production assertion.

By contrast, category memberships and template callers preserved in <code>archive-v1/index.json</code> are API-recorded implementation facts.

Do not collapse those evidence classes.

== Topic decomposition test ==

The first recovery supports the current V2 decomposition rather than a restoration of one universal Topic primitive.

Among classification-related source syntax, Topic-associated functions appear in multiple roles:

* domain / field signal — 43 occurrences;
* hierarchy or Topic-relation signal — 22 occurrences;
* entity/page-type use of <code>Topic</code> — 9 occurrences.

The exact counts include documentation/specification syntax and therefore are not production-schema counts. Their architectural value is qualitative: '''Topic was visibly performing several different jobs.'''

Current V2 separates those jobs into:

* [[Property:Domain|Domain]] — subject/field membership;
* Entity type — primary modeled identity;
* Category / Portal / index / outline — human navigation;
* explicit Relationships — graph semantics;
* Book Matter — reusable content anatomy.

The corpus evidence therefore strengthens the decision recorded in [[BITwiki:V1 ontology maturation]]: do not recreate the historical conflation merely because the label <code>Topic</code> was useful.

== What the evidence authorizes ==

The first corpus pass is strong enough to authorize several architectural conclusions:

# The separation between Entity type, Domain, navigation, Book Matter, epistemics/lifecycle, and Relationships is consistent with the historical evidence.
# The current twelve V2 exemplars are insufficient to decide the final Entity type or Domain vocabularies.
# V1 category membership cannot be migrated mechanically into V2 Category pages or semantic properties.
# V1 source-level type values mix ontology, page role, system role, and documentation role and therefore require contextual disposition.
# Pages that function as fragments, subpages, navigation sections, maintenance objects, or architecture documents must not be forced into the same identity schema as subject knowledge objects.
# The next ontology decision must come after V1 page-role and migration review, not before it.

== Next empirical pass: V1 page-role migration matrix ==

The next step is to review all 162 V1 Main pages as migration objects.

This is a '''migration-analysis lens''', not a new BITwiki ontology. Each page should be dispositioned by what job the historical artifact actually performs, for example:

* canonical subject-knowledge candidate;
* architecture / ontology / schema / governance / constitutional source;
* Book Matter / reusable section / subpage;
* navigation / portal / index / interface material;
* compatibility / legacy surface;
* maintenance / validation / test artifact;
* superseded or historical-only source;
* unresolved — requires deeper revision/content comparison.

Only the first group should directly pressure the base knowledge-object ontology. Other groups may pressure templates, Book Matter, navigation, lifecycle, provenance, or archive machinery instead.

For every candidate subject page, the review should then ask:

# What is the canonical knowledge object?
# What V1 type/category/domain signals exist, and which jobs were they actually performing?
# Which current V2 Entity type best models it?
# Which Domain value or values are justified?
# Does an existing relationship express secondary roles more cleanly than multiple types?
# Is a missing distinction repeated across several real pages?
# What source/revision lineage must be preserved during canonicalization?

That matrix is the evidence required before expanding the schema.

== Reproducibility ==

Run:

<pre>
python scripts/validate_v2.py
python scripts/inventory_corpus.py
</pre>

Pull-request validation runs both automatically and uploads the generated evidence files as a workflow artifact.

